<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">NKVD</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The NKVD (People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs) was the Soviet interior ministry which, from 1934, absorbed the political police and ran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> labour camps. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genrikh-yagoda">Genrikh Yagoda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov">Nikolai Yezhov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Lavrentiy Beria</a> it carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> of 1936–1938, in which about 680,000 people were shot, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a> of whole nationalities. It was renamed the MVD in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nkvd.jpg" alt="NKVD" width="320" height="529" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jgaray · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblema_NKVD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917 the Provisional Government dissolved the tsarist police, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> the Bolshevik government turned the Ministry of Internal Affairs into the People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs, the NKVD. Overwhelmed by its inherited duties, the government set up a secret political police, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a>, on 20 December 1917 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-dzerzhinsky">Felix Dzerzhinsky</a>, with the right to hold quick non-judicial trials and executions. The Cheka was reorganised in 1922 as the State Political Directorate and then as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogpu">OGPU</a>. In 1934 the NKVD became an all-Union body and absorbed the OGPU as its Main Directorate for State Security, taking over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> camps and the regular police.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genrikh-yagoda">Genrikh Yagoda</a> headed the NKVD from 1934 to 1936, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov">Nikolai Yezhov</a> from 1936 to 1938 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Lavrentiy Beria</a> from 1938 to 1946. Under Yezhov the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> reached its height: in 1937 and 1938 alone at least 1.3 million people were arrested and 681,692 were executed for crimes against the state, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> population grew by 685,201. Most of those executed were formally convicted by troikas, special tribunals, on very low standards of evidence, and the use of torture was sanctioned. Secret plans set quotas of victims by region, and under Order no. 00486 of 1937 the families of the repressed, including children, were also repressed. The Polish Operation of 1937 and 1938 alone resulted in the execution of 111,091 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>.</p><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the NKVD deported hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, Balts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> and millions of people of ethnic minorities from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, causing millions of deaths. Its Internal Troops, which grew from 15 rifle divisions to 53 divisions and 28 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a>, defended the rear and fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>. It also killed Polish prisoners in 1940, including in the Katyn massacre, and assassinated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in 1940. In March 1946 the commissariats became ministries and the NKVD became the Ministry of Internal Affairs, the MVD.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After Stalin's death in 1953 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a> halted the NKVD purges, and from the 1950s to the 1980s thousands of victims were rehabilitated, often with the formula that evidence of a crime was lacking rather than that they were cleared of all charges. Very few NKVD agents were ever convicted of violating anyone's rights. The labour of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> contributed to the Soviet planned economy, and the NKVD also ran sharashkas, special prisons where imprisoned scientists and engineers such as Sergey Korolev and Andrei Tupolev continued their work.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “NKVD” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Beria's struggle with First Secretary Aghasi Khanjian ended with Khanjian's killing on 9 July 1936; Beria's men, Amatuni Amatuni and the <b>NKVD</b> chief Khachik Mughdusi, arrested 1,365 people in ten months, among them the writers Charents, Bakunts and Zabel Yesayan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The political police, successively the Cheka, OGPU, <b>NKVD</b> and from 1954 the KGB, enforced conformity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>NKVD</b> then fought the UPA, killing over 103,000 rebels in 1944–1945; its commander Roman Shukhevych was killed in 1950, and the insurgency faded after 1954.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gulag, the Main Directorate of Correctional Labour Camps, was set up under the OGPU in 1930 and run by the <b>NKVD</b> from 1934.</blockquote><a href="/societies/soviet-union">Social Hierarchy of Stalin's Soviet Union</a></li><li><blockquote>They had been shot by the Soviet <b>NKVD</b> in 1940, a crime Moscow denied until 1990.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-13">13 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>NKVD</b> deported some 140,000 Polish citizens, many of them families of settlers and foresters, to Siberia and Kazakhstan.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
